findings.description:
Goal 1 demands "outputs verifiable against the current NumPy implementation" and "regression testing … mandatory". The NumPy path is float64 (`AgentState`, `BulkForce` return N×2 float64). Metal does not support float64, which is the doc's primary development GPU backend. CUDA and Vulkan paths are typically run in f32 for throughput.

A flocking system is chaotic, so trajectory equality is meaningless once precision differs. The doc doesn't define:
- the comparison unit (per-force output on a fixed state versus aggregate statistics);
- tolerances;
- how staggering (line 86), which deliberately changes behavior, is validated against the same baseline.

"Statistical properties are preserved" (line 86) is asserted with no metric (polarization, nearest-neighbor distance distribution, cluster counts, etc.). The cross-platform harness (line 110) is the last slice in the list, so the oracle arrives after the ports it should gate. Move a fidelity harness and its tolerances ahead of the Quadrants work.
